# Recovery

Backups in this bucket are encrypted with age (https://age-encryption.org).
Recommended: generate/update this file with `rbackup recovery-txt --org {{.Org}}`.

Source: https://github.com/kolontsov/rbackup
Runbook: https://github.com/kolontsov/rbackup/blob/main/docs/runbook.md

## Decrypt

If you have the identity file (`AGE-SECRET-KEY-...`):

`age -d -i identity.txt < backup.age > backup`

## .age2 (Double-Encrypted)

If the backup has a `.age2` extension, it has an additional passphrase layer:

1. Outer layer (scrypt/passphrase):

   `age -d < backup.age2 > backup.age`

2. Inner layer (age recipient):

   `age -d -i identity.txt < backup.age > backup`

Use the identity type that matches the recipient (`age1...` or `age1pq...`).

## If the Identity Was Derived (`derive-key`)

If the identity file was generated with `rbackup derive-key` (deterministic
derivation from passphrase + org), it can be re-derived manually. You need:

- Passphrase: <REQUIRED>
- Org: {{.Org}}

Compute Argon2id with these parameters:

- Salt: `rbackup-key-v1-{{.Org}}` (UTF-8 bytes)
- Time: {{.ArgonTime}}
- Memory: {{.ArgonMemory}} KB ({{.ArgonMemoryMB}} MB)
- Parallel: {{.ArgonThreads}}
- Hash len: {{.ArgonKeyLen}} bytes

X25519: the 32-byte output is the raw age identity.
Bech32-encode with HRP `age-secret-key-` and uppercase to get
`AGE-SECRET-KEY-1...` format.

ML-KEM-768/X25519 (`--pq`): the 32-byte output is the raw age PQ seed.
Bech32-encode with HRP `age-secret-key-pq-` and uppercase to get
`AGE-SECRET-KEY-PQ-1...` format.
Key expansion is done by age v1.3+ and follows HPKE DeriveKeyPair for
MLKEM768-X25519 (draft-ietf-hpke-pq).

## Backup Signature Verification (Optional)

If `x-rbackup-signature` metadata is present on the S3 object, the backup
was signed with the host's SSH Ed25519 key.

The signature is a base64-encoded raw Ed25519 signature (64 bytes) of the
`x-rbackup-sha256` hex string (the ASCII hex characters, not binary hash bytes).
You must compare the downloaded ciphertext SHA-256 to `x-rbackup-sha256`
before trusting the signature result.

Recommended: archive the public key(s) you trust
(`ssh-ed25519 AAAA...`) or a dedicated `allowed_signers_file` copy alongside
this document. Those files are what you use to verify signatures later.

1. Get object metadata: `aws s3api head-object --bucket BUCKET --key KEY`
2. Download the exact object/version you plan to decrypt
3. Compute SHA-256 of the downloaded ciphertext and compare it to `x-rbackup-sha256`
4. Extract `x-rbackup-signature` and base64-decode it (64 bytes raw Ed25519)
5. Verify: ed25519.Verify(hostPublicKey, []byte(sha256Hex), signature)

The signing key is typically `/etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key`.
Prefer archived public keys or an archived `known_hosts` file over live lookup.
If you did not archive them, an old `~/.ssh/known_hosts` entry may still be a
useful hint, but it is weaker and not deterministic policy. Do not treat live
`ssh-keyscan` output as something you trust during incident response.
